      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "MUC5AC mucin overproduction is a key feature of asthma as contributes to airway obstruction. The production of MUC5AC is regulated in part by signals from extracellular matrix via integrin pathways, but it remains largely unclear. We investigated the role of Akt, a typical signal transducer in the integrin pathway, in the regulation of MUC5AC production. When NCI-H292 human airway epithelial cells were cultured on laminin or Matrigel, we found that the activity of Akt was suppressed, as compared to control cells with upregulated MUC5AC production. In contrast, Akt was activated in cells cultured on type IV collagen with downregulated MUC5AC production. The Akt inhibitor induced upregulation of MUC5AC. In contrast, overexpression of active Akt induced downregulation of MUC5AC production. These results suggest that a signal from laminin or Matrigel induces upregulation of MUC5AC by suppressing Akt activity, whereas a signal from type IV collagen induces downregulation of MUC5AC, mediated by Akt activation."},
